config VPNHIDE
	bool "VPNHide in-tree VPN interface concealment"
	depends on NET && INET && IPV6 && PROC_FS
	default n
	help
	  Compile the VPNHide filtering logic directly into the kernel. It hides
	  active VPN network interfaces from selected Android apps (by calling
	  UID) by filtering ioctl, netlink dump, and /proc/net route responses
	  and refusing VPN-interface socket binds — the same detection vectors
	  the out-of-tree vpnhide_kmod covers, but attached at compile time
	  through source call-site hooks instead of kretprobes.

	  Choose this over the .ko on kernels where a loadable module cannot run
	  or cannot hook reliably: CONFIG_MODULES=n / CONFIG_INTEGRATE_MODULES=y,
	  CONFIG_KPROBES=n, or whole-program LTO that mangles hook-target symbols.

	  Configuration (target UID list + per-hook masks) is delivered at runtime
	  by the userspace activator through /proc/vpnhide_ctl using the shared
	  control protocol (docs/protocol.md); with no config written the hooks
	  are inert.

	  If unsure, say N.

config VPNHIDE_FS_HIDING
	bool "VPNHide filesystem path concealment"
	depends on VPNHIDE
	default y
	help
	  Also conceal a VPN interface's sysfs / proc-sys path nodes via VFS
	  call-site hooks (filename_lookup, do_filp_open, vfs_getattr,
	  iterate_dir). These sit on hot filesystem paths; a builder who does not
	  need path concealment can disable this to drop them entirely, leaving
	  only the network hooks.

	  If unsure, say Y.
